                  </origin>
                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_4">Canterbury, Kent. Benedictine
                        cathedral priory of Holy Trinity or Christ Church</orgName><p>'D(?)
                        secunda Gradus vi' on flyleaf (s. xiii ?).</p><p>R in top right corner of f.
                        1 and on last leaf.</p><p>Ambrosius de fide on flyleaf s. xii on specially
                        ruled line: 'uetus' and, after a space, 'libri nouem' have been added in
                        another hand or hands.</p><p>Medieval binding, skin over flat boards. E.
                        R.(?) at top of 2nd cover.</p><p>Chainmark in right bottom corner of 1st
                        cover.</p><p>Cf. ALCD, no 51 or 52 (one of these is St John's, Cambr.,
                        5).</p><p>'Liber Johannis Bayley ex dono dominae Annae Ailworth patruelis
                        1587 Sept. 17th'.</p><p>The form of the pressmark, in which D is an
                        elaborate and by itself illegible letter, is unusual, as is the occurence of
                        the letter mark at the end as well as at the beginning of the MS. On the
                        other hand, D. ii G. v or G. vi would be the correct pressmark for Ambrose,
                        which seems a strong argument in favour of Ch. Ch. provenance. And since
                        there are 2 copies of Ambrose de fide in its cat. we would expect one to be
                        marked uetus. Cf. T.C.C. B.3.31, B.3.33; C.C.C.C. 326; C.U.L. Kk.i.23; Royal
                        12.D.uv, Royal 10.A.xiii.</p><note type="MLGB3_medievalCatalogueNotes">cf.
                        cat. 51, 52</note><q type="pressmark">R, D.ii G.vi</q></provenance>
               </history>
